Because the term endpoint is used heavily across different tech sectors, the "types" vary depending on the context.
The types of endpoints can be broadly categorized into Network & Device Endpoints, API & Software Endpoints, Cloud Network Endpoints, and Clinical Trial Endpoints. [1, 2, 3] 
------------------------------
## Network & Device Endpoints
In computer networking and cybersecurity, an endpoint is any physical device that connects to and communicates with a network. These are categorized by their usage: [4] 

* End-User Devices: Laptops, desktops, smartphones, and tablets used by humans daily.
* Servers & Host Devices: Physical or virtual servers, databases, and mainframes that store data.
* IoT (Internet of Things) Devices: Smart thermostats, security cameras, smart watches, and medical equipment.
* Specialized Hardware: Point-of-Sale (POS) systems, retail kiosks, and automated teller machines (ATMs).
* Peripherals: Networked printers, scanners, and external storage drives. [2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9] 

------------------------------
## API & Software Endpoints
In software development, an API endpoint is a specific URL/URI path where a software application interacts with a server to perform operations. [10, 11, 12] 
## Categorized by Protocol/Architecture

* REST Endpoints: Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs) that use stateless HTTP methods (like GET or POST) to manage data.
* GraphQL Endpoints: A single, universal endpoint URL where clients query or mutate specific, customizable datasets.
* SOAP Endpoints: Direct web URLs utilizing XML-based messaging protocol for strict, enterprise-level communication.
* Webhooks (Reverse Endpoints): Endpoints provided by a client application that wait for an external server to push real-time event updates. [11, 13, 14, 15, 16] 

## Categorized by HTTP Method (CRUD) [17] 

* GET Endpoints: Used exclusively to read or retrieve data.
* POST Endpoints: Used to send data to create a new resource.
* PUT/PATCH Endpoints: Used to update or modify an existing resource.
* DELETE Endpoints: Used to remove a resource from the server. [10, 17] 

## Categorized by Security Lifecycle [18, 19] 

* Shadow Endpoints: Unmanaged and undocumented API URLs created by developers that pose security risks.
* Zombie Endpoints: Deprecated, abandoned, or outdated API endpoints that were never shut down. [18] 

